. In 1866, a ‘broad and deep ditch, four miles long’ was dug to bring water onto the Waihee (or Lewers) plantation in the same district. Manager of this plantation was Samuel T. Alexander who, at a later time, with Henry P. Baldwin constructed the much greater Hamakua ditch on the same island. References to\xa0irrigation\xa0are not numerous, but it is evident that the practice was adopted on other plantations'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 922, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 846})
